    "evidence_text": "Parallel processing in the mammalian retina\n\nThis is commonly referred to as parallel processing, and starts as early as the first synapse of the retina, the cone pedicle.\n\nHere, the molecular composition of the transmitter receptors of the postsynaptic neurons defines which images are transferred to the inner retina.\n\nWithin the second synaptic layer \u2014 the inner plexiform layer \u2014 circuits that involve complex inhibitory and excitatory interactions represent filters that select 'what the eye tells the brain'.\n\nSpecific deficit of the ON response in visual transmission by targeted disruption of the mGIuR6 gene\n\nTaking advantage of the restricted expression of metabotropic glutamate receptor subtype 6 (mGluR6) in retinal ON bipolar cells, we generated knockout mice lacking mGluR6 expression.\n\nThe homozygous mutant mice showed a loss of ON responses but unchanged OFF responses to light.\n\nThe results demonstrate that mGluR6 is essential in synaptic transmission to the ON bipolar cell and that the OFF response provides an important means for transmitting visual information.\n\nRESULTS Although the photoreceptor-mediated ERG component (a-wave) was normal in nob mice, the major response component reflecting postreceptoral neuronal activity (b-wave) was missing.",
